          "pause": true,
          "displayType": "poster",
          "buttonOnMobile": false,
          "label": "<p>What makes Shigella highly contagious compared to most other enteric pathogens?</p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>What makes Shigella highly contagious compared to most other enteric pathogens?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>It produces a heat-stable enterotoxin that persists in cooked food</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Heat-stable enterotoxins are characteristic of Staphylococcus aureus food poisoning, not Shigella.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>It requires an infective dose of only 10–100 organisms</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Correct! Shigella's infective dose of just 10–100 organisms — compared to approximately 10⁸ for V. cholerae — explains why direct person-to-person spread and outbreaks in schools and crowded settings are so common.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>It forms resistant endospores that survive in the environment for months</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Shigella is a non-spore-forming, non-motile Gram-negative rod. It does not survive harsh environments through spore formation.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>It spreads predominantly through contaminated aerosols</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Shigella spreads through the faeco-oral route — from person to person or via contaminated food and water — not by airborne droplets.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "Review the narration: Shigella needs only 10–100 organisms to cause disease, making person-to-person spread far easier than for other enteric pathogens that require millions of organisms."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Well done. The remarkably low infective dose is the single most important factor behind Shigella's high transmissibility."
                }
              ],
              "behaviour": {
                "enableRetry": true,
                "enableSolutionsButton": true,
                "enableCheckButton": true,
