          "buttonOnMobile": false,
          "label": "<p>Which virulence factor of Helicobacter pylori enables the bacterium to survive i</p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>Which virulence factor of Helicobacter pylori enables the bacterium to survive in the acidic environment of the stomach?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>CagA oncoprotein</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>CagA is injected into gastric epithelial cells where it disrupts tight junctions and activates cancer-promoting signalling pathways, but it does not protect the bacterium against gastric acid.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>VacA cytotoxin</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>VacA creates pores in gastric epithelial cells, causing vacuolation and cell death. It contributes to tissue damage but does not shield the bacterium from acid.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Urease enzyme</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Correct! Urease converts urea to ammonia and CO₂, creating a local alkaline microenvironment that neutralises gastric acid around the bacterium. This enzyme is so central that nearly every diagnostic test for H. pylori — including the rapid urease test and the urea breath test — is based on detecting its activity.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Sheathed flagella</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Sheathed flagella provide motility through the viscous gastric mucus layer, helping the bacterium reach the epithelial surface, but they do not provide acid resistance.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "Remember, H. pylori produces an extremely powerful urease that neutralises stomach acid around itself — this is so distinctive that almost every diagnostic test for this organism is built around detecting it."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Exactly. Urease is the cornerstone of H. pylori's survival strategy and the basis for most diagnostic tests."
                }
              ],
              "behaviour": {
                "enableRetry": true,
                "enableSolutionsButton": true,
                "enableCheckButton": true,
