          "displayType": "poster",
          "buttonOnMobile": false,
          "label": "<p>Which serological marker specifically indicates active hepatitis B virus replica</p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>Which serological marker specifically indicates active hepatitis B virus replication and high infectivity?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Anti-HBs antibody</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Anti-HBs indicates immunity — either from successful vaccination or from recovery after natural infection. Its presence is protective, not a marker of ongoing replication.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Anti-HBc IgG</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Anti-HBc IgG appears after the acute phase and persists indefinitely, indicating past exposure or a chronic carrier state. It does not specifically reflect whether the virus is actively replicating.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>HBeAg</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Correct! HBeAg is a secreted form of the core antigen and serves as a direct marker of active HBV replication. Its presence signals high viral load and high infectivity. Seroconversion from HBeAg to anti-HBe indicates that replication is declining — a favourable prognostic sign.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>HBsAg</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>HBsAg confirms active infection — either acute or chronic — but by itself it does not tell you whether the virus is actively replicating or whether the patient is highly infectious.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "Revisit the markers: HBsAg = active infection, anti-HBs = immunity, anti-HBc IgM = acute phase, HBeAg = active replication and high infectivity, anti-HBe = declining replication."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Correct. HBeAg is the key replication marker, and its clearance (seroconversion to anti-HBe) is a treatment milestone."
                }
              ],
              "behaviour": {
                "enableRetry": true,
                "enableSolutionsButton": true,
                "enableCheckButton": true,
                "type": "auto",
