        {
          "duration": {
            "from": 938.5187916666667,
            "to": 948.5187916666667
          },
          "pause": true,
          "displayType": "poster",
          "buttonOnMobile": false,
          "label": "<p>A healthcare worker tests positive for anti-HBs at 15 mIU/mL and negative for al</p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>A healthcare worker tests positive for anti-HBs at 15 mIU/mL and negative for all other hepatitis B markers including anti-HBc. What does this serological profile indicate?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Acute hepatitis B infection in the window period</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>During the window period, HBsAg has cleared but anti-HBs has not yet appeared. The only detectable marker would be anti-HBc IgM — which is negative in this case, ruling out the window period.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Chronic hepatitis B carrier state</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>A chronic carrier would be HBsAg-positive and anti-HBc-positive. Both are negative here, ruling out chronic carriage.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Immunity from past natural hepatitis B infection</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Recovery from natural infection produces both anti-HBs (indicating immunity) and anti-HBc (indicating prior exposure to the core antigen). The absence of anti-HBc rules out prior natural infection.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Immunity from hepatitis B vaccination</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Correct! The hepatitis B vaccine contains only HBsAg, so it elicits only anti-HBs antibody. Anti-HBc appears only after natural infection with the whole virus. Anti-HBs alone at ≥10 mIU/mL confirms vaccine-induced protective immunity.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "The key principle: the vaccine contains only HBsAg, so it can only produce anti-HBs. Anti-HBc requires exposure to the core antigen, which occurs only during natural infection. Anti-HBs alone = vaccinated; anti-HBs plus anti-HBc = recovered from infection."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Well done. Recognising that anti-HBs without anti-HBc equals vaccination — not natural infection — is a critical distinction for clinical practice."
                }
              ],
              "behaviour": {
